The == operator checks whether the memory addresses of two objects are equal, while the equals() method checks whether the content of the objects are equal. The == operator is often used to compare primitive values such as int and double, while the equals() method should be used to compare complex objects such as String and Date. The equals() method should always be preferred over == when comparing objects in Java. Furthermore, it is generally a good idea to override the equals() method in your classes so that it can be used in a meaningful way. 
